14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"August 30–September 4, 1862","place":"Northern Virginia and the approaches to Washington, from Manassas and Centreville through Chantilly and Fairfax Court House to Washington and Alexandria","result":"Federal command transition after a Confederate campaign victory. Pope’s Army of Virginia withdrew into the Washington defenses and was absorbed into McClellan’s Army of the Potomac. McClellan took command of the forces defending the capital on September 2 and rapidly restored order, while Lee ended his pursuit and turned toward the Potomac for the Maryland Campaign","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The aftermath of Second Manassas and McClellan’s return to command","note":"The episode reviews the August campaign, assigns responsibility for the Federal defeat, follows Pope’s retreat, and explains Lincoln’s September 2 decision. It ends as Lee begins crossing the Potomac into Maryland on September 4."},{"label":"Second Manassas campaign strength","value":"75,696 United States troops and 48,527 Confederates","note":"These are campaign participation figures used by the episode and the U.S. Army Center of Military History. They are not the number simultaneously engaged on one battlefield."},{"label":"United States campaign losses","value":"1,724 killed, 8,372 wounded, and 5,958 missing","note":"The three categories total 16,054. The episode rounds this to about 16,000 while noting that published totals vary according to the dates and actions included."},{"label":"Confederate campaign losses","value":"1,481 killed, 7,627 wounded, and 89 missing","note":"The three categories total 9,197, commonly rounded to about 9,200 in the episode’s account."},{"label":"Lee’s strategic objective","value":"Defeat Pope before the Army of the Potomac could unite with him","note":"Lee used Jackson’s wide turning movement to force Pope away from the Rappahannock and strike the Federal rear. Longstreet followed and delivered the decisive August 30 counterattack."},{"label":"Jackson’s flank march","value":"54 miles in 36 hours in the episode’s account","note":"Jackson reached Manassas Junction, destroyed Federal stores, and withdrew to ground near the earlier Bull Run battlefield. The episode credits this strategic movement while sharply criticizing his tactical performance at Groveton and on August 30."},{"label":"Longstreet’s August 30 attack","value":"The largest attack Lee launched during the war","note":"The assault crushed Pope’s left but lost cohesion across difficult ground. Federal defenders at Chinn Ridge and Henry Hill kept the defeat from becoming the destruction of Pope’s army."},{"label":"Chantilly / Ox Hill","value":"An indecisive September 1 action that stopped Jackson’s attempt to cut the retreat","note":"Kearny’s and Stevens’s divisions fought Jackson in a severe thunderstorm. Both Federal division commanders were killed, but Pope’s army continued toward Washington."},{"label":"Pope’s command","value":"Army of Virginia dissolved after its return to Washington","note":"Its formations were integrated into the Army of the Potomac. Pope received orders on September 6 to proceed to Minnesota, ending his 74 days in command of the Army of Virginia."},{"label":"Fitz John Porter prosecution","value":"Court-martialed in 1863 for alleged disobedience during Second Manassas","note":"Pope made Porter the principal target of his post-campaign accusations. Porter was dismissed from the Army; after a later review rejected the original findings, Congress restored his commission in 1886."},{"label":"Halleck’s position","value":"General-in-chief over two poorly coordinated Federal armies","note":"Halleck had ordered the Army of the Potomac away from the Peninsula. The episode argues that his indecision and inability to compel rapid cooperation helped leave Pope exposed to Lee."},{"label":"McClellan’s role before the defeat","value":"Controlled the pace at which Army of the Potomac units reached Pope","note":"McClellan opposed the Peninsula withdrawal, regarded Pope as a rival, and delayed or resisted orders to send forces forward from Alexandria. Lincoln described his conduct as bad but still judged him the available commander most able to restore the army."},{"label":"Crisis at Washington","value":"Defeat produced public alarm and severe army demoralization","note":"Pope’s beaten formations and Army of the Potomac units converged on the capital with low confidence in their senior commanders. Lee’s army remained close enough to make defense and reorganization urgent."},{"label":"Lincoln’s decision","value":"McClellan placed in command of the capital’s defenses on September 2","note":"Lincoln and Halleck visited McClellan that morning and asked him to take charge of the forces falling back into Washington, including Pope’s army. The restoration did not make McClellan general-in-chief."},{"label":"McClellan’s first arrangements","value":"Alert garrisons, clear roads, collect stragglers, and secure trains","note":"In a 12:50 p.m. report to Lincoln, McClellan described guards and food on the roads, cavalry moving Pope’s trains, wagons placed away from troop routes, and Abercrombie’s brigade ordered to Chain Bridge."},{"label":"Cabinet opposition","value":"Stanton and Chase protested the restoration","note":"Four cabinet members had supported a memorandum urging McClellan’s removal. Lincoln acknowledged the accusations against him but maintained that McClellan could reorganize the army and bring it out of chaos."},{"label":"Episode title","value":"John P. Hatch announced McClellan’s return within Pope’s hearing","note":"As McClellan met the retreating column, Hatch called out that McClellan was back in command and asked for three cheers. The episode uses the announcement as its title."},{"label":"Army reaction","value":"Retreating soldiers cheered McClellan’s return","note":"Contemporary observers who did not share McClellan’s politics nevertheless recorded his exceptional popularity with the Army of the Potomac. The episode treats that confidence as the practical reason Lincoln’s choice worked in the immediate emergency."},{"label":"Immediate reorganization","value":"Separate formations became a workable field army within days","note":"McClellan integrated the Army of Virginia’s units with the Army of the Potomac, restored discipline and supply movement, and prepared the combined force to respond to Lee."},{"label":"Next campaign","value":"Lee began crossing the Potomac into Maryland on September 4","note":"The transfer of command at Washington therefore became the opening Federal response to the Maryland Campaign, not merely an administrative aftermath to Second Manassas."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Quartermaster: Montgomery C. Meigs, Lincoln’s General, Master Builder of the Union Army” by Robert O’Harrow Jr.","note":"The official episode page and transcript identify this biography of the Union quartermaster general as the recommendation."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Hennessy, Irvin McDowell, Philip Kearny, Montgomery C. Meigs, and Robert O’Harrow Jr.","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Hennessey,” “Ervin MacDowell,” “Phil Carney,” “Migs,” and “Robert O’Hara Jr.” The guide uses the standard historical spellings and the author spelling supplied by the official episode page."}],"maps":[{"title":"Retreat into the Washington defenses","caption":"After the August 30 defeat, Pope withdrew through Centreville. Jackson’s attempt to cut the route produced the September 1 fight at Chantilly, after which the Federal column continued through Fairfax Court House toward Washington and Alexandria. Lee then turned northwest toward Leesburg. Roads, waterways, routes, and locations are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, U.S. War Department map, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep180-retreat-to-washington.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Pope’s retreat from Manassas through Centreville, Chantilly, and Fairfax Court House into the Washington defenses, with Lee turning toward Leesburg."},{"title":"McClellan’s defensive response on September 2","caption":"McClellan reported alerting the garrisons, placing guards and food on the roads, using cavalry to clear Pope’s trains, moving wagons away from troop routes, and ordering Abercrombie’s brigade to Chain Bridge. Arrows indicate the generalized direction of the army’s return, not exact unit routes. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"George B. McClellan’s September 2, 1862 report to Abraham Lincoln, U.S. War Department map, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep180-washington-response.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Washington, Alexandria, Chain Bridge, Fairfax Court House, and McClellan’s first measures for receiving Pope’s retreating army."},{"title":"“McClellan is our man”","caption":"This Harper’s Weekly engraving, published on August 2, 1862, shows soldiers in camp cheering McClellan. It predates the September 2 restoration and does not depict Hatch’s announcement, but it is contemporary evidence of the personal attachment to McClellan that shaped Lincoln’s decision.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","alt":"An 1862 Harper’s Weekly engraving of Union soldiers in camp cheering George B. McClellan."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:11","audioTime":71,"title":"The episode turns to the campaign aftermath","detail":"The hosts move from Second Manassas and Chantilly to the command crisis surrounding Pope, Halleck, Lincoln, and McClellan."},{"time":"1:36","audioTime":96,"title":"Campaign casualties are clarified","detail":"The episode notes that totals vary, then gives about 16,000 United States losses and 9,200 Confederate losses for the campaign."},{"time":"2:50","audioTime":170,"title":"Lee’s first full campaign in command is assessed","detail":"The review distinguishes Lee’s mid-campaign assumption of command during the Peninsula Campaign from his direction of the Northern Virginia Campaign from the outset."},{"time":"3:57","audioTime":237,"title":"Jackson begins the wide turning movement","detail":"Lee sends Jackson around Pope’s right on August 24; the episode credits the 54-mile march with making the Second Manassas victory possible."},{"time":"4:46","audioTime":286,"title":"Jackson’s battlefield performance is criticized","detail":"The episode contrasts Jackson’s strategic movement with his handling of the Groveton fight and his delayed advance on August 30."},{"time":"5:45","audioTime":345,"title":"Longstreet’s decisive contribution is reviewed","detail":"Longstreet’s large August 30 assault broke Pope’s left, though terrain and command control caused successive blows rather than one coordinated impact."},{"time":"6:35","audioTime":395,"title":"Lee’s command decisions are summarized","detail":"Lee waits on August 29 and attacks decisively on August 30, coming close to destroying a United States army without completing that objective."},{"time":"7:34","audioTime":454,"title":"Pope reaches the Washington defenses","detail":"Chantilly stops Jackson’s final turning attempt, and Pope completes the retreat from Centreville toward the capital."},{"time":"8:32","audioTime":512,"title":"Lincoln reacts to another defeat","detail":"The episode uses Lincoln’s comment to John Hay to frame the discouragement in Washington after Second Manassas."},{"time":"9:07","audioTime":547,"title":"McDowell’s Chinn Ridge decision is revisited","detail":"McDowell’s removal of Reynolds’s division shortly before Longstreet struck is identified as one of the Federal army’s gravest tactical errors."},{"time":"9:32","audioTime":572,"title":"Pope makes Porter the principal target","detail":"The episode explains why Fitz John Porter, a McClellan ally, became the focus of Pope’s accusations after the campaign."},{"time":"10:28","audioTime":628,"title":"Porter’s eventual restoration is noted","detail":"After years contesting the court-martial, Porter receives a favorable review and has his commission restored by Congress in 1886."},{"time":"11:12","audioTime":672,"title":"Halleck’s coordination failure is examined","detail":"The general-in-chief struggles to bring Pope’s and McClellan’s armies together while Lee exploits the separation."},{"time":"13:33","audioTime":813,"title":"McClellan controls a critical campaign variable","detail":"The speed with which Army of the Potomac units join Pope determines how long Lee can operate against the Army of Virginia alone."},{"time":"14:52","audioTime":892,"title":"McClellan’s delays are reviewed","detail":"The episode describes a slow Peninsula evacuation and resistance to sending troops from Alexandria during Pope’s crisis."},{"time":"15:54","audioTime":954,"title":"Lincoln uses the available commander","detail":"Despite condemning McClellan’s recent behavior, Lincoln concludes that he is the officer most able to meet the emergency."},{"time":"16:34","audioTime":994,"title":"Responsibility for the defeat is assigned","detail":"The episode holds Halleck and McClellan partly responsible but identifies Pope’s decisions as the principal cause of the Federal calamity."},{"time":"20:06","audioTime":1206,"title":"Pope is ordered to Minnesota","detail":"The September 6 assignment removes Pope from the eastern command after the Army of Virginia’s dissolution."},{"time":"21:26","audioTime":1286,"title":"Washington confronts alarm and demoralization","detail":"Reports from the capital and the retreating ranks show fear for Washington and a collapse of confidence in the army’s leadership."},{"time":"23:58","audioTime":1438,"title":"Lincoln weighs cabinet opposition","detail":"Stanton seeks a court-martial, Chase urges extreme punishment, and four cabinet members support removing McClellan."},{"time":"24:51","audioTime":1491,"title":"Lincoln and Halleck visit McClellan","detail":"On the morning of September 2, they ask McClellan to take charge of the forces retreating into the Washington defenses."},{"time":"25:15","audioTime":1515,"title":"McClellan accepts the command","detail":"McClellan later writes that Lincoln asked him to resume command and do the best possible, and that he accepted without conditions."},{"time":"26:08","audioTime":1568,"title":"The cabinet receives Lincoln’s decision","detail":"Stanton and Chase protest, while Lincoln insists that McClellan is the one commander who can reorganize the demoralized army."},{"time":"27:15","audioTime":1635,"title":"McClellan rides out to meet the retreat","detail":"In dress uniform and riding Dan Webster, McClellan encounters Pope, McDowell, and the returning columns."},{"time":"27:46","audioTime":1666,"title":"Hatch announces McClellan’s return","detail":"John P. Hatch calls to the soldiers that McClellan is back in command and asks for three cheers within Pope’s hearing."},{"time":"28:08","audioTime":1688,"title":"The cheering spreads through the column","detail":"Soldiers and observers describe an immediate change in morale as news of McClellan’s restoration moves along the road."},{"time":"29:41","audioTime":1781,"title":"The merged army is made ready again","detail":"Within days McClellan reorganizes and integrates the formations into a force capable of responding to Lee."},{"time":"30:05","audioTime":1805,"title":"Lee crosses into Maryland","detail":"The first Army of Northern Virginia units begin crossing the Potomac on September 4, opening the next campaign."},{"time":"30:24","audioTime":1824,"title":"The episode’s book recommendation is given","detail":"The hosts recommend Robert O’Harrow Jr.’s biography of Quartermaster General Montgomery C. Meigs."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"Commander in chief","subordinates":"Made the September 2 decision to place McClellan over the forces defending Washington despite strong cabinet opposition"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General-in-chief, United States Army","subordinates":"Transmitted national strategy and orders; accompanied Lincoln to McClellan and remained senior to him after the command change"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Forces in and around Washington; Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Received Pope’s retreating formations, controlled the capital’s garrisons and approaches, and reorganized the combined force for field operations"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John Pope","command":"Army of Virginia during the retreat","subordinates":"Directed the withdrawal from Second Manassas and Chantilly; relinquished his forces to McClellan before assignment to Minnesota"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Irvin McDowell","command":"III Corps, Army of Virginia","subordinates":"Retreated with Pope and met McClellan on September 2; his August 30 decisions remained a focus of post-battle criticism"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John P. Hatch","command":"1st Division, III Corps, Army of Virginia","subordinates":"Had earlier been removed from cavalry command by Pope; publicly announced McClellan’s return to the passing troops"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John J. Abercrombie","command":"Brigade, Darius N. Couch’s division","subordinates":"Ordered to Chain Bridge as part of McClellan’s immediate defensive arrangements on September 2"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Ended the pursuit after Chantilly, concentrated near Chantilly on September 2, and then turned the army toward Leesburg and Maryland"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Left Wing, Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Conducted the campaign’s wide flank march and the final attempt to intercept Pope at Chantilly"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Right Wing, Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Directed the massive August 30 assault that broke the Federal left at Second Manassas"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander restored to command of the forces defending Washington on September 2","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep180-george-mcclellan.jpg"},{"name":"John Pope","role":"Army of Virginia commander defeated at Second Manassas and relieved after the retreat","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep180-john-pope.jpg"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"General-in-chief responsible for coordinating Pope and McClellan and carrying out Lincoln’s command decision","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep180-henry-halleck.jpg"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President who restored McClellan to command of the forces around Washington during the emergency","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who defeated Pope and then shifted toward Maryland","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander whose turning movement shaped the campaign and whose troops fought at Chantilly","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander whose August 30 assault decided Second Manassas","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Irvin McDowell","role":"Army of Virginia corps commander criticized for removing Reynolds’s division from Chinn Ridge","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"Army of the Potomac corps commander accused by Pope and later dismissed after court-martial","side":"United States"},{"name":"Edwin M. Stanton","role":"Secretary of war and McClellan opponent who protested the restoration","side":"United States"},{"name":"Salmon P. Chase","role":"Treasury secretary who strongly opposed McClellan’s return","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Hay","role":"Presidential secretary whose notes preserve Lincoln’s private judgments during the command crisis","side":"United States"},{"name":"John P. Hatch","role":"Cavalry officer whose announcement of McClellan’s return supplied the episode title","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip Kearny","role":"Army of the Potomac division commander killed while helping stop Jackson at Chantilly","side":"United States"},{"name":"Isaac I. Stevens","role":"Army of Virginia division commander killed while leading troops at Chantilly","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"Maj. Gen. Isaac I. Stevens","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 1st Division, IX Corps","outcome":"Killed at Chantilly on September 1 while leading his division against Jackson’s attempt to cut Pope’s retreat route."},{"name":"Maj. Gen. Philip Kearny","side":"United States","role":"Commander, 1st Division, III Corps, Army of the Potomac","outcome":"Killed at Chantilly on September 1 after riding into Confederate troops while checking his line during the thunderstorm."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep180-retreat-to-washington.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the Federal retreat from Manassas through Centreville, Chantilly, and Fairfax Court House to Washington and Alexandria.","caption":"The fight at Chantilly stopped Jackson’s last effort to cut the Federal retreat. Pope’s army continued into the capital’s defenses while Lee turned toward Leesburg.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Army Center of Military History, U.S. War Department map, Robert Knox Sneden map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep180-washington-response.svg","alt":"Schematic map of McClellan’s September 2 arrangements on the western approaches to Washington.","caption":"McClellan’s first report after taking charge describes an immediate traffic, supply, garrison, and straggler-control response as Pope’s troops returned.","source":"George B. McClellan’s September 2, 1862 report to Abraham Lincoln, U.S. War Department map, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","alt":"An 1862 wood engraving of Union soldiers cheering George B. McClellan in camp.","caption":"Published one month before McClellan’s restoration, this Harper’s Weekly engraving documents the army attachment that Lincoln relied upon after Second Manassas. It is not a view of the September 2 encounter.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep180-george-mcclellan.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general George B. McClellan.","caption":"George B. McClellan resumed command around Washington on September 2 and quickly reorganized the returning formations for the Maryland Campaign.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep180-john-pope.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general John Pope in uniform.","caption":"John Pope’s Army of Virginia ceased to exist as an independent army after its retreat. Pope was ordered to Minnesota on September 6.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep180-henry-halleck.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general-in-chief Henry W. Halleck.","caption":"Henry W. Halleck remained general-in-chief. McClellan’s restored authority covered the troops in and around Washington, not Halleck’s national post.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 180 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/01/29/civil-war-podcast-episode-180/"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: Battle of Second Manassas","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mana/learn/historyculture/second-manassas.htm"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: United States order of battle at Second Manassas","url":"https://www.nps.gov/mana/learn/historyculture/union-order-of-battle-at-second-manassas.htm"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: The Attack Continues and Chantilly aftermath","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/the-attack-continues.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary: Chantilly / Ox Hill","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va027"},{"label":"Manassas National Battlefield Park: Second Manassas aftermath","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/information-panel-aftermath.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Antietam campaign timeline","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/timeline.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Civil War campaign summaries","url":"https://history.army.mil/Research/Reference-Topics/Army-Campaigns/Brief-Summaries/Civil-War/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Virginia Campaigns, March–August 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-5.pdf"},{"label":"Primary source: McClellan’s September 2, 1862 report to Lincoln","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1818700/"},{"label":"National Archives: Fitz John Porter court-martial and review records","url":"https://www.archives.gov/publications/prologue/1995/fall/honorable-reports-2.html"},{"label":"Primary map: U.S. War Department map of northeastern Virginia and Washington","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/lva0000020/"},{"label":"Primary map: Robert Knox Sneden’s Centreville position, August 31, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/gvhs01.vhs00107/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: “McClellan is our man” engraving","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2003653567/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: George B. McClellan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018669440/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John Pope portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666476/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Henry W. Halleck portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666431/"}]}}]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","“McClellan is our man”",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open “McClellan is our man” at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","alt":"An 1862 Harper’s Weekly engraving of Union soldiers in camp cheering George B. McClellan.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"“McClellan is our man”"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"This Harper’s Weekly engraving, published on August 2, 1862, shows soldiers in camp cheering McClellan. It predates the September 2 restoration and does not depict Hatch’s announcement, but it is contemporary evidence of the personal attachment to McClellan that shaped Lincoln’s decision."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Commander in chief-President Abraham Lincoln",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Commander in chief"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"President Abraham Lincoln"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Made the September 2 decision to place McClellan over the forces defending Washington despite strong cabinet opposition"}]]}],["$","div","General-in-chief, United States Army-Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"General-in-chief, United States Army"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Transmitted national strategy and orders; accompanied Lincoln to McClellan and remained senior to him after the command change"}]]}],["$","div","Forces in and around Washington; Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Forces in and around Washington; Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Received Pope’s retreating formations, controlled the capital’s garrisons and approaches, and reorganized the combined force for field operations"}]]}],["$","div","Army of Virginia during the retreat-Maj. Gen. John Pope",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of Virginia during the retreat"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. John Pope"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Directed the withdrawal from Second Manassas and Chantilly; relinquished his forces to McClellan before assignment to Minnesota"}]]}],["$","div","III Corps, Army of Virginia-Maj. Gen. Irvin McDowell",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"III Corps, Army of Virginia"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Irvin McDowell"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Retreated with Pope and met McClellan on September 2; his August 30 decisions remained a focus of post-battle criticism"}]]}],["$","div","1st Division, III Corps, Army of Virginia-Brig. Gen. John P. Hatch",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Division, III Corps, Army of Virginia"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. John P. Hatch"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Had earlier been removed from cavalry command by Pope; publicly announced McClellan’s return to the passing troops"}]]}],["$","div","Brigade, Darius N. Couch’s division-Brig. Gen. John J. Abercrombie",{"className":"command-entry","children":["$L20","$L21","$L22"]}]]]}],"$L23"]}],"$L24"]]]}]
17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"losses","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Important leaders killed or wounded"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"loss-list","children":[["$","article","Maj. Gen. Isaac I. Stevens",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Isaac I. Stevens"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander, 1st Division, IX Corps","."]}]," ","Killed at Chantilly on September 1 while leading his division against Jackson’s attempt to cut Pope’s retreat route."]}]]}],["$","article","Maj. Gen. Philip Kearny",{"children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"side union","children":"United States"}],["$","h3",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Philip Kearny"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":["Commander, 1st Division, III Corps, Army of the Potomac","."]}]," ","Killed at Chantilly on September 1 after riding into Confederate troops while checking his line during the thunderstorm."]}]]}]]}]]}]
18:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L25","/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep180-mcclellan-cheered.jpg","alt":"An 1862 wood engraving of Union soldiers cheering George B. McClellan in camp.","caption":"Published one month before McClellan’s restoration, this Harper’s Weekly engraving documents the army attachment that Lincoln relied upon after Second Manassas. It is not a view of the September 2 encounter.","name":"An 1862 wood engraving of Union soldiers cheering George B. McClellan in camp.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L25","/images/ep180-george-mcclellan.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep180-george-mcclellan.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general George B. McClellan.","caption":"George B. McClellan resumed command around Washington on September 2 and quickly reorganized the returning formations for the Maryland Campaign.","name":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general George B. McClellan.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L25","/images/ep180-john-pope.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep180-john-pope.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general John Pope in uniform.","caption":"John Pope’s Army of Virginia ceased to exist as an independent army after its retreat. Pope was ordered to Minnesota on September 6.","name":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general John Pope in uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}],["$","$L25","/images/ep180-henry-halleck.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep180-henry-halleck.jpg","alt":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general-in-chief Henry W. Halleck.","caption":"Henry W. Halleck remained general-in-chief. McClellan’s restored authority covered the troops in and around Washington, not Halleck’s national post.","name":"Civil War-era glass-negative portrait of United States general-in-chief Henry W. Halleck.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}]]}]
